  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Golden Rule & Force-Push Safety",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Server-side force-push protection is the only enforceable form of the golden rule. Conventions do not survive a bad night."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why A Rebase Breaks The Push",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never rebase commits that left your repository",
            "After rebase, local and remote share no commits",
            "Normal push is rejected as non-fast-forward",
            "`--force` overwrites a teammate's push from 30s ago",
            "`--force-with-lease` errors instead of overwriting"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git push --force-with-lease \\",
            "  --force-if-includes   # Git 2.30+"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "First Five Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mistake",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "rebased a shared branch",
              "coordinate, or revert instead"
            ],
            [
              "ours/theirs inverted",
              "zdiff3, read the base"
            ],
            [
              "committed conflict markers",
              "hook + git diff --check"
            ],
            [
              "--theirs on modify/delete",
              "git rm or git add explicitly"
            ],
            [
              "reset --hard mid-rebase",
              "always rebase --abort"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# .git/hooks/pre-commit",
            "git diff --cached --check || exit 1",
            "grep -rn '^<<<<<<< ' . && exit 1"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Getting Work Back",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "commits lost after rebase",
              "reflog, then reset --hard"
            ],
            [
              "orphans past the reflog",
              "git fsck --lost-found"
            ],
            [
              "bad merge, unpushed",
              "reset --hard ORIG_HEAD"
            ],
            [
              "bad merge, already pushed",
              "git revert -m 1 <sha>"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reflog                 # find HEAD@{n}",
            "git reset --hard HEAD@{5}  # pre-rebase tip"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Traps With Merges",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "re-merge after a revert",
              "revert the revert, then merge"
            ],
            [
              "squashed, then merged again",
              "delete and re-branch"
            ],
            [
              "dirty tree blocks rebase",
              "stash, or rebase.autoStash"
            ],
            [
              "same conflict every commit",
              "rerere before you start"
            ],
            [
              "pull invents merge commits",
              "pull.rebase or pull.ff"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Re-merging after a revert brings back nothing: the merge base already accounts for those commits, so Git sees no work to do."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Golden Rule & Force-Push Safety (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Teammate Recovery",
          "text": "Someone force-pushed a rebase under you? `git fetch`, then `git rebase --onto origin/feature <old-upstream> local-work`. Hard reset only if you have nothing local worth keeping."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pick Your Tool",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Reach for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "not diverged, want it in",
              "merge --ff-only"
            ],
            [
              "diverged and shared",
              "git merge"
            ],
            [
              "local, nothing pushed",
              "git rebase main"
            ],
            [
              "messy WIP before PR",
              "git rebase -i"
            ],
            [
              "feedback on an old commit",
              "--fixup + --autosquash"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Reach for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "need one commit only",
              "cherry-pick -x <sha>"
            ],
            [
              "undo something public",
              "git revert, never rebase"
            ],
            [
              "built on the wrong base",
              "rebase --onto new old br"
            ],
            [
              "many replayed conflicts",
              "rerere.enabled first"
            ],
            [
              "confused which side",
              "merge.conflictStyle zdiff3"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Worked Example: Wrong Base",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Branch `feat` was cut from `spike` by mistake. It must sit on `main` instead, keeping only its own three commits."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git log --oneline spike..feat  # 3 commits",
            "git rebase --onto main spike feat",
            "git push --force-with-lease"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--onto main` is the new parent",
            "`spike` is the old base, excluded",
            "`feat` is the branch being moved",
            "Every replayed commit gets a new SHA"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Integration Policy",
          "ink": "blue"
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Undo Recipes & Cheat Sheet",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Team shape",
            "Policy"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "long-lived, release branches",
              "merge commits"
            ],
            [
              "small team, patch series",
              "rebase then ff"
            ],
            [
              "messy WIP, one feature",
              "squash merge"
            ],
            [
              "trunk-based, short branches",
              "rebase or squash"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Rebase locally, merge publicly — the standard compromise",
            "Delete a branch after squashing it, or it re-conflicts",
            "Enforce policy in branch protection, not in a wiki"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Configure Once",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git config --global rerere.enabled true",
            "git config --global rebase.autoStash true",
            "git config --global pull.rebase true",
            "git config merge.conflictStyle zdiff3"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Recovery Reflex",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git reflog` first — always, before panicking",
            "`git reset --hard <good-sha>` second",
            "Nothing committed in ~90 days is truly lost",
            "Uncommitted work is the only work you can destroy"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Zero-Risk Moves",
          "items": [
            "merge: never rewrites anything",
            "revert: adds, never removes",
            "--ff-only: refuses to invent history",
            "reflog: read-only, free"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Whole Handbook In One Line",
          "text": "Merge records what happened; rebase edits the story; fast-forward means there was no story to reconcile. Choose by who else can already see the commits."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of GIT REBASE & MERGE. Most Git pain is one confusion: merge preserves what happened, rebase rewrites it into what you wish had happened — and the SHAs pay the price. This handbook goes down to the object level: three-way diff internals, the ort strategy, patch-ids, index stages, why ours/theirs invert during rebase, and a decision table for every integration situation. Save it for the next time a rebase halts on commit 4 of 10.",
